{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfMjnFYhnU1hrMrNBbNnt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```json\n[\n  {\n    \"unitKey\": \"20240818-power-q08\",\n    \"questionNumber\": 8,\n    \"part\": null,\n    \"status\": \"FIX\",\n    \"textIssues\": [],\n    \"choiceIssues\": [],\n    \"answerIssues\": [],\n    \"explanationIssues\": [\n      \"choiceExplanations[1] says the offset keeps conductors apart even when their swing grows 'due to wind, galloping, etc.' That misstates what an offset is for. An offset is a horizontal displacement between the upper and lower conductors. It mainly prevents contact from vertical movement: sleet jump when ice sheds, and the vertical amplitude of galloping. It is not a measure against sideways swing caused by wind. Suggested replacement: '正しい。上下に配列された電線の水平位置をずらす（オフセットを設ける）ことで，着氷雪脱落時のスリートジャンプやギャロッピングにより電線が上下方向に大きく動いても，上下の電線どうしが接触・混触しにくくする方法として用いられている。'\"\n    ],\n    \"figureIssues\": [],\n    \"sourceIssues\": [],\n    \"verifiedEvidence\": [\n      \"The question stem and all five choices match the official page 8 (T1-D 011) word for word, including '風速10 m/s'.\",\n      \"The official answer table (R6 first half, Power, Q8) gives 3, which matches officialAnswer '3'.\",\n      \"The galloping explanation is correct. Asymmetric ice with wind produces aerodynamic instability and a low-frequency, large-amplitude oscillation of roughly 0.1–1 Hz. Spacers stop bundled conductors from twisting, so the ice stays asymmetric and galloping occurs more easily than on a single conductor. The claim that it 'rarely occurs on bundled conductors' is therefore backwards, which confirms (3) as the wrong statement.\",\n      \"The explanations for (2) aeolian vibration (Kármán vortices, high frequency, small amplitude, dampers), (4) subspan oscillation (wake instability of the leeward subconductor, strong wind around 10 m/s or more) and (5) sleet jump (rebound after ice sheds) are all correct.\",\n      \"The question has no figure. Empty figureUrls is appropriate, and the problem can be understood from text alone.\",\n      \"officialReferenceUrls is empty, so there are no independent claims needing an outside source. reviewedFromPage [11] is consistent with the T1-D 011 code on the page image.\"\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"unitKey\": \"20240818-power-q09\",\n    \"questionNumber\": 9,\n    \"part\": null,\n    \"status\": \"PASS\",\n    \"textIssues\": [],\n    \"choiceIssues\": [],\n    \"answerIssues\": [],\n    \"explanationIssues\": [],\n    \"figureIssues\": [],\n    \"sourceIssues\": [],\n    \"verifiedEvidence\": [\n      \"The preamble (definitions of external and internal overvoltage), the instruction line and all five choices match the official pages 9 and 10 (T1-D 012 and 013) word for word.\",\n      \"The official answer table (R6 first half, Power, Q9) gives 1, which matches officialAnswer '1'.\",\n      \"The overall explanation and the explanation for (1) are correct. Insulation coordination means rationally matching the arrester protection level to each piece of equipment's insulation strength so the system is safe and economical. It does not mean reducing the external overvoltage itself.\",\n      \"(2) matches the definition of a lightning arrester (limit the overvoltage, interrupt the follow current, self-restore). Calling the residual voltage during discharge the 'limiting voltage' is also correct.\",\n      \"(3) correctly describes the design approach for overhead lines: flashover (including back-flashover) is tolerated, and the fault rate is kept down with overhead ground wires and lower grounding resistance.\",\n      \"(4) correctly states that internal overvoltages stay at a few times the normal phase-to-ground voltage (switching surges up to about 3 times), so withstanding them in full is economical.\",\n      \"(5) correctly states that substation equipment relies on arresters and that BIL is set with a margin above the protection level based on the arrester's limiting voltage.\",\n      \"The question has no figure, so empty figureUrls is appropriate. officialReferenceUrls is empty, so no outside source check is needed. reviewedFromPage [12,13] is consistent with the T1-D 012 and 013 codes on the page images.\"\n    ]\n  }\n]\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3969,"cache_read_input_tokens":162992,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3969},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b8f0bdd5-d258-4b29-92fc-a7461c369c67","uuid":"75987bab-96a3-40a9-9e7a-35194e9b27ad","timestamp":"2026-09-24T05:48:47.029Z","request_id":"req_011CfMjnEe99ZmMJ9bqbKYAN"}
